1. `ls` : type this to list all content available in current directory in CLI. By default `ls` 
    gives in alphabetical order.
    
2. `pwd` : whenever we are doing any operation(creation , deletion , rename of a file) this will
    happens inside the current directory and `pwd` tells us in which directory(folder) we are 
    currently working in . Generally when we open a fresh terminal by default we are in home 
    directory(folder) but we are not then type `cd` command to get to home directory.
	
3. `how file system works in computer` : filesystem organizes in a way of upside down tree . root 
    directory (represented by `/` forward slash) is the main directory which holds the other directories.
    In this we have `bin` which has builtin programs stored , `Users` which has our personal data and 
    files , `data` which contains other miscellaneous files , `temp` this holds the programs which 
    don't require to hold for long time. And in `Users` there will be different directories for 
    different users who are using this machine `/Users/abhinav` this will contains all files of 
    abhinav `/Users/raman` this one for raman and so on.

4. `forward slashes '/' has two meanings`: when used as prefix of the path of whole directory then 
    this represents `root directory` and when used other than starting of path this acts as separator.

5. `ls -F` : Use to get distinction among files , executables, directories and so in a directory. In 
    linux `/` Directory , `*` program file (executable) ,`@` symbolic link, `=` socket , `|` FIFO/ named pipe.

6. `cd ~/Desktop`: this will take you in `Desktop` directory in home folder (as in linux `~` represents home 
    folder)  and `cd ~/Desktop/20-10-2026` this will take you to `20-10-2026` directory inside `Desktop folder` 
    and on (use `cd` with folder name like `cd ~` this will take you to home directory  ) to go inside directory
    (make sure that directory should be present in that particular directory) and to come back use `cd` like `cd 
    ~/Desktop` this will take you to `Desktop` from `20-10-2026` directory or to get one level back `cd ..` this 
    will take one level back.  and by typing only `cd` you will go to home directory.

7. `ls --help`: `ls` is not just used to print the list of available contents in a particular directory combining 
    `ls` with some functions gives differnt outputs you get all combinations of possible things by using `ls --help` 
    this will list out all functionalities we can perform with `ls` using `--` and what each could be used for like 
    `ls --size` this will allocated size of each sub file or whatever content our current directory.

8. `--help`: almost all builtin commands of terminal supports `--help` which tell all possible functionality can be 
    achieved using that particular command and what is the use of that command.

9. `When to use short and long options` : when you used help for any command and access all commands then you will 
    found that for any functionality there is one shorter version and one longer version . Use shorter version when
    you are running that particular command in terminal directly wherease use longer version when you are curating
    any particular file that will run multiple commands automatically.

10. `man ls` : this is also very similar to `ls --help` but here you will get all things in a page manner so you 
    can go back forward using up and down arrows and `q` to quit, `b` to reach top of the page(this words like page up)
    , spacebar to reach bottom of the page(this works like page down) , `/` then type what you wanted to search in the
    page and then all words of what you searched get highlighted, `n` to move forward on the highlighted words and 
    `shif+n` to move backward. and even man is applicable for almost all builtin commands in terminal like `--help` 
    as man only convers `ls--help` in pager manner. 

11. `ls -t` : this will sort your list in chronological order of time(newest edit will short first).

12. `ls -r` : this used to reverse the alphabetical order.

13. `(ls -r -t) or (ls -t -r) or (ls -rt) `: we use any one from these to sort our list in reverse order with respect
    to time we edited that directory or file or any program that is present in that particular directory. And using 
    in any order will works same because command line treats as same means that using any one command line will 
    concluse reverse the order of list on the basis of time of edited . 

14. `Even you can use all ls functionality on a different directory other then the directory on which you are working` 
    : mans `ls Desktop` this will list all content of `Desktop` directory `ls -F Desktop` this will classify all 
    contents of the `Desktop`. means `ls` + `functionality of ls you wanted to use` + `on which directory you wanted 
    to use`. But make sure you that your current must exist that particular directory because by default our directory
    is home and which has Desktop so this works fine otherwise we will encounter an error. 

15. `cd`: this is not just change directory it will change working directory in our terminal . many terminals don't 
    provide our current working directory when we change it using `cd` but use `pwd` this will print that.
